{"sounds":{"intro":"Amharic is the working language of Ethiopia. It is written in the Ge'ez script (fidel), where each symbol is one consonant plus one vowel. Every word here has three layers: the English, the Latin letters, and the Ge'ez writing.","rows":[["t' / ch' / p' / ts'","ejective sounds: pop the consonant out with a sharp burst of air, with no breath","ጠ ጨ ጰ ጸ"],["q","an ejective k made deep in the throat","ቀ"],["sh / zh / ny / j","like \"ship\", \"vision\", \"canyon\", \"judge\"","ሸ ዠ ኘ ጀ"],["e","like \"e\" in \"bed\" (first and fifth orders)","ሰ ሴ"],["u","like \"oo\" in \"food\"","ሱ"],["i","like \"ee\" in \"see\"","ሲ"],["a","like \"a\" in \"father\"","ሳ"],["ə","a short, weak vowel (sixth order), often almost silent","ስ"],["o","like \"o\" in \"more\"","ሶ"],["w","after a consonant it rounds the lips (a labial form)","ቋ ኳ ጓ"]],"notes":["Each Ge'ez symbol has seven forms (orders), one for each vowel. The consonant stays the same shape, and the vowel changes the shape slightly. Learn the consonants first.","Amharic is written left to right, and a gap is used to separate words. Verbs end each sentence. \"You\" has three forms: to a man, to a woman, and formal.","Doubled consonants (gemination) change the meaning but are not shown in the script. The Latin shows them doubled."]},"numbers":[{"id":"0-to-10","title":"0 to 10","items":[["zero","zero","","ዜሮ"],["one","and","","አንድ"],["two","hulet","","ሁለት"],["three","sost","","ሶስት"],["four","arat","","አራት"],["five","amist","","አምስት"],["six","sidist","","ስድስት"],["seven","sebat","","ሰባት"],["eight","simint","","ስምንት"],["nine","zet'egn","","ዘጠኝ"],["ten","asir","","አስር"]]},{"id":"11-to-20","title":"11 to 20","items":[["eleven","asra and","","አስራ አንድ"],["twelve","asra hulet","","አስራ ሁለት"],["thirteen","asra sost","","አስራ ሶስት"],["fourteen","asra arat","","አስራ አራት"],["fifteen","asra amist","","አስራ አምስት"],["sixteen","asra sidist","","አስራ ስድስት"],["seventeen","asra sebat","","አስራ ሰባት"],["eighteen","asra simint","","አስራ ስምንት"],["n